---
title: "Accessibility diagnostics demo"
---

This page deliberately contains accessibility problems so both the preview's
client-side a11y audit (the dev-menu panel) and the static a11y gate (`validate_a11y`,
run by every build and by `build --check-only`) have something to flag. It lives in
`corpus/diagnostics/`, which is exempt from the corpus clean-render guards. Each located
finding (the ones tied to a block) jumps to its source line on click, like any other
diagnostic.

## A section with a raw image

<img src="logo.png">

The raw `<img>` above has no `alt` attribute, so a screen reader can't describe it
(a decorative image should use `alt=""`).

![image](logo.png)

The image just above has a non-empty but useless `alt="image"` — it names the medium,
not the content, so it passes the missing-alt check yet still tells a screen-reader user
nothing.

#### A subsection that skips a heading level

This heading is an `h4` directly under the `h2` above, skipping `h3`.

The accessible-name rules that used to live here (an icon-only link, an unlabelled
`<button>`, and the `[role=button|link|tab]` variants of both) were cut on 2026-08-08
with the rest of the diagnostics contraction, along with WCAG 2.5.3's label-in-name
mismatch. The preview's client-side audit is where an interactive-element defect is
found now.
